The slot model (the why), verified:

```js
// React tracks hooks by POSITION in the call sequence:
// Render 1: [state:a] [state:b] [effect:c]   ← slots fill in order
// Render 2: [state:a] [state:b] [effect:c]   ← same order = same slots

// THE VIOLATION — a conditional hook:
function Bad({ showCount }) {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');        // slot 1 — always
  if (showCount) {
    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);    // slot 2 — SOMETIMES
  }
  useEffect(() => {});                        // slot 2 or 3 depending on the if!
}
// Render with showCount=true:  [name][count][effect]
// Render with showCount=false: [name][effect]
// The effect's slot now reads COUNT's state — hooks misalign.
// React detects it: 'Rendered fewer hooks than expected' → CRASH.
```

The two rules stated precisely:
1. Top level only — no if, no loops, no callbacks, no early returns before hooks
2. React functions only — components and custom hooks (functions named use*)

The correct pattern for the conditional need (what people are usually TRYING to do):
```js
function Good({ showCount }) {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);   // ALWAYS called — slot stable
  if (!showCount) return null;           // conditional RENDER, not hook
  return <div>{name} {count}</div>;       // early return AFTER hooks is fine
}
```
The loop need → fix by giving each iteration its own component (its own slot list) or composing hooks into a custom hook with a stable count.
